  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nClearing skies and warm temperatures have returned to the area this \nafternoon. Temperatures will warm a little bit more to the 80s to \nlow 90s with heat indices up to the upper 80s and 90s. A few \nlocations along the Missouri River Valley are expected to see heat \nindices touch 100F this afternoon before temperatures cool as the \nsun begins to set. A few light showers/sprinkles persist across \nparts of southwest Minnesota but will push east of the area over the \nnext hour or two. Attention then turns to the evening timeframe \nwhere additional chances for rain look to return to the area. \nShowers and some storms look to develop across western Nebraska and \nsouthwest South Dakota. These showers will push eastwards into an \nincreasingly stable airmass sitting over the forecast area. At the \nsame time, vertical shear values will be weakening as well. Still, \ncan't rule out the potential for an isolated strong storm or two \nbetween 9pm to 3am. Should a strong storm or two develop, it could \nproduce large hail up to the size of a quarter (1 inch) and damaging \nwinds to 60 mph IF the storm can tap into the boundary layer. The \nmost likely location to see these potentially stronger storms is \nalong and west of the James River. Elevated showers look to persist \nthrough the rest of the night.\n\nSunday will be a similar day with high temperatures once again \nexpected to warm to the upper 80s and 90s. This will again push heat \nindices to the 90s with a few locations along the Missouri River \nagain seeing heat indices touch 100F during the afternoon timeframe. \nA stronger shortwave trough will be passing mainly through Montana \nthroughout the day. This wave however will take on a negative tilt \nand push into northeastern Montana and northwestern North Dakota. \nSevere storms are possible in this area but is much more \nquestionable for our area. Especially as the cold front tied to this \nwave pushes into the forecast area due to limited \nmoisture/instability and capping just ahead of the front. Thus, have \nleft PoPs dry for Sunday evening and night.\n\nThe cold front will push through the area Sunday night through \nMonday morning. With the front just south of the area, Monday looks \nto be a continuation of warm and dry conditions with highs warming \nto the 80s to up to about 90F. Chances for rain look to return to \nthe forecast on Tuesday as a weak shortwave trough pushes into the \nNorthern Plains. The mass response from this wave will return the \npreviously mentioned boundary northwards as a warm front. The \nensembles generally show a 50-80% chance for rainfall totals to \nexceed a tenth of an inch. This will likely be contingent on where \nthe front sets up and if the cap can be broken or not. As of now, \nlatest machine learning (ML) guidance shows a 5-10% chance for \nstrong to severe storms on Tuesday. Tough to say if trends will hold \nover the next few days so will keep an eye on Tuesday going forward. \n\nChances (20-40%) for rain persist for the rest of the week along \nwith a continuation of above average temperatures. Highs look \nto remain in the mid to upper 80s Wednesday through Saturday. ML\nguidance shows low chances (<= 5%) for strong to severe storms.\nThus, could see a continuation for strong to severe storms for \nthe rest of the week but confidence in specific details remains \nlow.",
